# CROSS-SURFACE DESIGN SYSTEM — ADMIN × ACCOUNT

One Hearst design language. Multiple business semantics.

## Doctrine

- **Foundations are shared** across Hearst Connect: color / surface / line / radius
  intent / elevation / typography / numeric typography / motion / state semantics /
  chart language. The single authority is `src/styles/tailwind.css` (`@theme` → `:root`).
- **Shared foundations ≠ shared component tree.** Product areas may expose different
  semantic primitives over the same tokens.
- **Admin semantic primitives** (`surfaceBox`, `Panel`, `DashCard`, `BentoGrid`) must
  **not** be imposed on account surfaces without a real semantic reason. The account
  is not required to become admin.
- **Account semantic primitives** (`UserShell` / `.shell`, `StatTile`, `CompositionRail`,
  `MovementTimeline`, `CapacityMeter`, `DriftLedger`) must **not** redefine foundational
  color / material / motion semantics. They compose tokens; they do not fork them.
- **Extract a shared foundation only when the semantic role is actually shared** — and
  only when there is more than one real consumer. Do not create a universal abstraction
  (a `UniversalHearstGrid`, a single-use elevation token) merely to collapse duplicate
  class strings or a one-off magic number.

## Token authority — no local fallbacks

The `@theme` tokens resolve at `:root` under the `.dark` root layout on every product
route. A `var(--color-…, #fallback)` in a business CSS module therefore never renders
its fallback — it only encodes a second, drifting copy of the token. Reference tokens
bare: `var(--color-…)`. (The `--font-satoshi` font stack keeps its progressive chain —
that is a font fallback, not a color.)

## Placement

- `BUSINESS SEMANTICS OWN DOM` · `LAYOUT OWNS PLACEMENT`.
- A component does not own page placement; a dataset does not own page geometry.

This is a permanent architectural rule, not a one-time migration.
